If standard models were latently hyperbolic, the instrument should find an excess on their image features, where the premise is read. Does it, and does anything survive on class centroids, where hierarchy lives?

\paragraph{The premise does not survive calibration.} We read it first on per-image features, roughly a thousand stratified training images per cell for CIFAR-100 and DTD and all twelve backbones, where the raw reading lies in the low band the literature reports. Calibrated, it is not genuine in {{SL_WITHIN}} of 24 cells, and where it is genuine the excess is small and concentrated in the self-supervised family, and that family's structure is the angular tree of Section~\ref{sec:content}, which cosine collects. A low raw reading on image features is therefore what a random cloud of the same dimension and spectrum produces, up to a small margin. This is the reading that motivates hyperbolic methods, and it does not survive calibration as stated (Table~\ref{tab:census}, last two columns). Class centroids, where hierarchy lives, read differently, and we turn to them next. % expR62_samplelevel_record.csv

\paragraph{Clustered structure is the rule.} Figure~\ref{fig:excess} and Table~\ref{tab:census} give the census of record. Almost every cell sits below its matched null, and in 49 of the 72 the gap is one that chance does not produce. On ImageNet and CIFAR-100, the two sets with a real hierarchy, the count is 18 of 24. The structure belongs to no single family or architecture: every family contributes genuine cells, two self-supervised ResNets read the same as the ViTs, and resampling the images behind each centroid does not move the reading. What does move it is the label set: the smallest backbone is genuine on one dataset only, the flat datasets sit at the resolution limit of ten classes, and a class-count control shows that the reading follows the hierarchy of the labels rather than their number (Appendix~\ref{app:csweep}). A star, however, would pass this test too, which is the subject of the next paragraph. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, expR59_imagenet_bootstrap_summary.csv, expR45_convnet_rows.csv, expR60_c_sweep_record.csv

\paragraph{The count survives resampling.} The census null accounts for replicate variability but not for image resampling or the estimator's seeds. Folding all three into one spread, or repeating the census on thirty resampled centroid sets, leaves between {{R11_LO}} and {{R11_HI}} of the 72 cells genuine. On ImageNet and CIFAR-100 the count stays between {{R11T_LO}} and {{R11T_HI}} of the 24, and the cells that move are on the transfer sets (Appendix~\ref{app:robust}). % expR66_joint_sensitivity_summary.csv

\paragraph{A star passes the census.} The excess measures structure beyond second moments, and a pure star already has such structure. A synthetic star of thirty clusters with no hierarchy scores an excess as deep as any real cell. We call this the \emph{star caveat}. The excess therefore measures how classes are arranged, not the geometry they are embedded in, and a negative excess says clustered, not deep. Whether the arrangement is deeper than a star is a separate question, which needs a test of its own (Appendix~\ref{app:star}). That test is the subject of the next paragraph. % expR42_star_calibration.csv

\paragraph{Depth is certified on ImageNet and not on CIFAR-100.} The depth test compares the real centroids with a matched star: Gaussian hubs at the real hub radius and, within each cluster, a Haar sample with the cluster's own covariance, both read under a null that randomizes the hubs and keeps every cluster intact. Because the null keeps the clusters, the test certifies hierarchy above the labelled clusters only. On ImageNet the real cloud is more hierarchical than its matched star in {{N_IN}} of 12 backbones, three supervised ViTs and DINOv2-L, and never less. The other eight are not detected, which is not the same as not hierarchical. CIFAR-100, with clusters of a few points, lies outside the regime the test covers and its readings stay unvalidated. An earlier isotropic star produced false alarms and is withdrawn (Figure~\ref{fig:depth}a). How much depth the test can see on real clouds is the subject of the next paragraph. % expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR55b_depth_power_leafframe.csv, expR55_depth_power.csv

\paragraph{On real clouds the test is conservative and weak.} We measured the test on the real ImageNet clouds themselves, replacing the hub arrangement by an implanted two-level tree of graded strength while keeping every within-cluster offset, and the census cannot tell the implanted tree from the star. The test raises no false alarm, declaring none of the sixty zero-strength runs hierarchical. A clean implanted tree is detected in {{R9B_NDET_PHRASE}}, and the misses are a matter of noise level: the within-cluster spread of the real clouds is {{RATIO_LO}} to {{RATIO_HI}} times their between-hub spread, the synthetic sweep covered ratios below one, and once the real offsets are shrunk into that range every backbone is detected. The test is therefore conservative and weak at ImageNet's noise level: its positive verdicts stand, and a backbone it does not certify is not thereby flat. A trained control, a ViT-B fine-tuned with a hierarchical objective, is inconclusive, as Appendix~\ref{app:star} reports (Figure~\ref{fig:depth}b). Whether imposing the geometry creates detectable depth is the next question. % expR64b_wn30.csv, expR64b_wn30_summary.csv, expR65_hier_finetune.csv

